===============================================================================
KNIGHT SHELL - DEVELOPERS DOCUMENTATION
===============================================================================

Version: 0.8.0
Language: C (Standard C99/C11)
Repository: https://github.com/NickHubDev/Knight-Shell.git

This document is intended for developers who want to understand the internal 
structure of Knight-Shell, compile it from source, or contribute to its core 
development.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PROJECT STRUCTURE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The codebase is organized as follows:

- main.c       : Starter point, in this file we only call the Kloop()
                 function.

- src/         : Source files containing core logic, built-in commands, 
                 and utility functions.

- headers/     : Headers of all files are created here for been included
                 on the library.h file which recolects all the header files.

- shell/       : Additional environment/shell subsystem components.

- Makefile     : Compilation automation script for GCC/Clang.

- install.sh   : Installer script for standard users.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. COMPILATION AND BUILDING
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

As a developer, you might prefer to bypass the 'install.sh' script and manage 
the build process manually using the provided Makefile.

Requirements:

- A C compiler (gcc by default used in the project)

- GNU Make

To build the executable:

$ make

This will compile the source files and generate the 'knight' binary in the 
actual directory.

To implant the program in your binnaries instead:

$ make production

This will create the binnary file named as "knight" and throw
it into your /usr/bin/ folder, so now you can use "knight" command 
in your shell to run the shell.

To clean build artifacts (object files and binaries):

$ make clean

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. UNDER THE HOOD & FUTURE IMPLEMENTATIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Architecture: 

  The shell reads user input, parses arguments, forks a child 
  process, and uses execvp (or similar) to run system binaries. Built-in 
  commands are executed directly in the parent process.
  
- Configuration Subsystem: 

  We are currently developing a custom parser for 
  ".ksh" (Knight Shell Script) configuration files. This will allow lightweight 
  environment styling and custom alias bindings. 

  By the time of this files are beeing development you can use ".sh" import to
  a "config.sh" that might be created at the "$HOME/.config/ksh/" path.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. HOW TO CONTRIBUTE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

We welcome code optimizations, memory leak fixes, and new features!

Workflow:

1. Fork the repository on GitHub.

2. Create a feature branch ($ git checkout -b feature/amazing-feature).

3. Ensure your C code is well-formatted and follows standard memory 
   management practices (always free() allocated buffers).

4. Verify that 'make' compiles without warnings.

5. Commit your changes ($ git commit -m 'Add some amazing feature').

6. Push to the branch ($ git push origin feature/amazing-feature).

7. Open a Pull Request detailing your changes.

